  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 241 AM MDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nThe active monsoon pattern of the past couple weeks that has\nbrought beneficial rainfall looks to be finally shifting the\nrest of the week. The H5 ridge placement centered on the Four\nCorners has been efficient in drawing moisture northward, and\nwill continue today to trigger some shower activity for WY.\nToday, the focus of moisture looks to be more across southern\nWY, where the best chances for precipitation are setting up this\nafternoon. Some brief drying is noted across northern WY today,\nso shower activity will be more limited, and focused on higher\nelevations. The lower moisture for most of the state will bring\ndecreased cloud cover, allowing temperatures to jump back well\nabove normal. Areas east of the Divide will jump into the mid to\nupper 90s, while southern WY, with the better shower chances and\ncloud cover in the afternoon, will still reach the mid to upper\n80s. \n\nA final push of deeper monsoon moisture surges northward \ntonight, along with another shortwave wrapping around the ridge.\nThis will bring much better chances for numerous showers and \nthunderstorms for the day Wednesday. Rainfall amounts will be\nbeneficial again, but storm motions will be high enough to \npreclude most flooding concerns during the afternoon.\n\nOnce this shortwave sweeps northeast Wednesday night, the H5\nridge bulges to the west, effectively shutting the door on the\nmonsoon flow, and instead drawing much drier air from the Great\nBasin into the state. This will effectively shut down rain\nchances for the rest of the week into the upcoming weekend. The\nridge also bulges back to the north, bringing increasing\ntemperatures across the state. Probabilities for 100 degree\ntemperatures this weekend currently sit at >60% east of the\nDivide, and around 30% west of the Divide, including the greater\nJackson region.",
